// Portuguese word list -- background tier, part 4 of 4 (712465 words).
// Full grammatical expansion (conjugated/declined forms), built to bring
// Portuguese up to the same depth as Polish and Arabic. Source: the real,
// GPL-licensed Hunspell dictionaries for European Portuguese (pt-PT,
// official/curated) and Brazilian Portuguese (pt-BR, VERO project),
// affix-expanded via the standard Hunspell `unmunch` tool.
//
// Scoping decision: pt-BR's affix rules are far more generative than
// pt-PT's (allowing deep stacking of prefixes and superlative suffixes,
// e.g. "sesquiinternacionalizabilissimamente"), which would have produced
// an unreasonably bloated ~11.9M-word dictionary dominated by obscure
// derivational curiosities rather than words a player would plausibly
// encounter. Instead: pt-PT's full expansion (2,679,157 words, capped at
// 15 letters -- the maximum a Scrabble board can hold, same cap used for
// Polish) forms the deep tier, and only pt-BR's own dictionary headwords
// not already covered by pt-PT (206,641 words) were added as
// Brazilian-specific extras, rather than pt-BR's full mechanical
// expansion. Final scope: 2,885,798 words before merging with the
// existing cleaned base.
//
// Cleaning: removed 15 confirmed unambiguous vulgar/offensive forms
// (e.g. words derived from "foder", "caralho", a homophobic slur) found
// among the expanded forms. Rescued two genuine false positives a cruder
// filter would have caught -- "pirocaína" and "pirocatecol" are real
// pharmaceutical/chemistry terms, not derivatives of vulgar slang despite
// the shared substring. Common/ambiguous words already accepted in the
// existing base dictionary (e.g. "porrada", "cacete", "corno") were left
// untouched for consistency with earlier cleanup decisions.
//
// Split across 4 files so each stays under GitHub's 25MB web-upload
// limit -- all 4 are fetched in parallel and merged in once every part
// has arrived, same pattern as Polish and Arabic.
